1 NOTE {geni:about_me} DAR Ancestor #: A091081
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT VITAL RECORDS: Birth dates for children and marriage date for Thomas and Abigail come from a undated, undocumented copy of a family bible record in the possession of ( at one time) J. F. Peterson, this bible record has surfaced ma
2 CONC ny time in many places.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1790 US Census - Newberry Co., South Carolina Thomas Peterson
2 CONT # White Males Under 16:  3
2 CONT # White Males Over 16:  1
2 CONT # White Females:  5 Total # Household:  9
2 CONT
2 CONT 1810 US Census: Newberry Co., South Carolina Thomas Peterson Free White Males: 10-15=2, 16-25=2, 45 Over=1 Free White Females: 10-15=1, 16-25=2, 45 Over=1
2 CONT # Household Under 16: 3
2 CONT # Household Over 25: 2
2 CONT
2 CONT 1820 US Census: Union, Union Co., SC Thomas Peterson Free White Males: Under 10=1, 10-15=1, 26-44=1 Free White Females: Under 10=2, 26-44=1
2 CONT
2 CONT THOMAS PETERSON is the first Peterson to whom records have been found that connects him to the Peterson's who came to Dallas Co., Arkansas in the 1850's from Alabama. His exact date of birth is unknown, but it was around 1755. He 
2 CONC married Abigail Richardson, born ca 1755, September 1776, who was possibly the daughter of John Richardson?
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas had a sister named Elizabeth, who married Amos Richardson, a brother of Abigail. They were married in either Virginia or South Carolina. They lived mostly in the Edgefield District, S.C. They became parents of eleven known 
2 CONC children. Abigail died June 15, 1825, and Thomas, September 12, 1827, both in Edgefield. Thomas did leave a Will, dated March 1, 1827, and it was probated in the same year in Edgefield, S.C.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Peterson's will dated March 1, 1827, and was probated on March 21, 1827.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Name of God Amen I Thomas Peterson of Edgefield District State of South Carolina Being well stricken in years and infirm in body yet Perfect in mind and memory do make and ordain and declare this instrument which is my Last
2 CONC  will and Testament. I will to my beloved son William Peterson Sixty five Acres of Land joining John Henson and Star--- Land
2 CONT
2 CONT The Balance of my Lands I will to my Beloved sons Thomas Peterson and James Peterson to divide as they see proper I also will to my beloved grandson Milton Perkins one grey horse colt and one three year old heiffer with calf I als
2 CONC o will to Elizabeth Perkins one wheel and cards also one wheel cards and Loom and reell to Casandra Perkins.
2 CONT
2 CONT I also wish all the rest of my property su---- house hold and kitchen furniture and stock of all kind to be Sold and Equally divided amongst my children also one negro girl named Lucy to be Sold and divided I also wish Milton Perk
2 CONC ins to have the half of the corn and cotton that himself and Lucy can raise on the Land  the present year the Ballance to be sold and divided equally.
2 CONT
2 CONT I do appoint my sons David Peterson and James Peterson Executors of this my will in witness whereof I do set my hand and seal this the first day of March 1827 and in the fifty first year of the independence of the United States of
2 CONC  America. Test Casandra Perkins Elizabeth Perkins (signed ) Thomas Peterson William Adams
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas is registered in the DAR records as a patriot of the Revolutionary War as one who sold and furnished supplies to the Military by selling flour. Mrs Vera Mae Baker Holder researched this information for a number of years and
2 CONC  was officially certified in the National Society of the Daughters Of The American Revolution, June 11, 1993. The National No. is 753153. Any other descendants of Thomas may also join.
2 CONT -----------
2 CONT April 25-June 24, 1781, Thomas Peterson served as a private in the Brittish Army in the America Revolution. He served in Capt SamuelRowarth's Co. King's Rangers. He signed up in Augusta, GA. SOURCE:Vol 1, page 73 Loyalists in the 
2 CONC Southern Campaign of the RevoluntionaryWar, by Clark, 1981.
2 CONT -------
2 CONT 9th Generation descendent Vera Mae Baker Holder joined the DAR in 1992 through her Revolutionary Ancestor THOMAS PETERSON. Her application states that THOMAS PETERSON resided at Camden District, South Carolina during the American 
2 CONC Revolution and that while acting in the capacity of patriotic service supplied 200 pounds of flour to Militia in 1779.
2 CONT
2 CONT Feb 22, 1779 received by Lieut Henry Caffey for Lieut Col. Early Kershaw Reg't of Militia. 200 weight of flour. Original copy in Data F. C. Stub Indents of Claims Against S.C. for Rev. War. Book U - W #540 - Lib. U.
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of Thomas and Abigail Richardson Peterson were: 1).   Naomi Peterson was born February 2, 1778; 1/m Daniel Goggans, 2/m John Glenn.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2).   William Peterson was born April 11, 1780; died October 22, 1866. He was married to Nancy Mangam, born August 30, 1786; died January 11, 1869. Both are buried in Mangam Cemetery, 8 miles NW of Silverstreet, S.C.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3).   David Thomas Peterson was born May 15, 1780; died April 7, 1827. Married Mary "Polly" Turner. David was a Baptist Minister. Mary was also married to William Langford.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4).   Thomas Peterson was born March 1, 1784
2 CONT
2 CONT 5).   Elizabeth Peterson was born July 2, 1786; Married August 4, 1801 to Jeremiah (Jerry) Goggans. Jerry was born June 30, 1776; died April 11, 1807. Had one child named Daniel, before moving to Ohio, where Jerry died shortly aft
2 CONC er. Elizabeth returned to Newberry, S.C. with their son. (above information from "Annals of Newberry", originally published 1892.)
2 CONT
2 CONT 6).   Cassandra Peterson was born May 13, 1788; died November 22, 1866. Married to James Gorgons. Both are buried in the Old Shiloh Cemetery near Nixburg, S.C.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7).   Mary Peterson was born June 2, 1790. Married name was Perkins. Husband's first name may have been William.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8).   John Peterson was born July 15, 1792.
2 CONT
2 CONT 9).   Brazilla (Basil) Peterson was born September 10, 1794. Married Charlotte Richardson Strothers. Charlotte was Brazilla's first cousin. She was a granddaughter of Amos and Elizabeth Peterson Richardson. Her father was David Ri
2 CONC chardson. She was married first to Patrick Ardough, October 1807, and then to a George James Strothers. Brazilla was her third husband. (above information from will of Amos Richardson).
2 CONT
2 CONT 10).   James Foy Peterson was born October 21, 1796; died June 10, 1881. Married November 24, 1822 to Rebecca Williamson. James was also a Baptist Minister; he pastored the Red Bank Church for 42 years. He and his wife were parent
2 CONC s of 6 known children; Elizabeth, Abner, Abigail R., Thomas, James Franklin, and Bazilla.
2 CONT
2 CONT 11).   Abigail Peterson was born October 3, 1798. Married Willis Burnett, born 1802, son of Hezekiah and Elizabeth Burnett.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1790 US Census - Newberry Co., SC Thomas Peterson 1, 3, 5, 0, 0
2 CONT
2 CONT 1800 Newberry Co., S. C. Census THOMAS PETERSON - no statistics given
2 CONT
2 CONT 1810 US Census - Newberry Co., SC Peterson, Tho  0,2,2,0,1,0,1,2
2 CONT
2 CONT 1820 Census - Union Co., SC Peterson, Thomas 1,1,0,0,1,0,2,0,0,1,0,0,1
2 CONT
2 CONT We find the names of Thomas and Abigail Peterson on the rolls of the Bush River Baptist Church from the earlist church roll which is dated prior to the Revolutionary War.
2 CONT
2 CONT ______
2 CONT
2 CONT Important sources: (1) Minutes of the Good Hope Baptist Church, Saluda Co., SC - Abigal (Richardson) Peterson died 5 Jan 1825, Edgefield Co., SC
2 CONT
2 CONT (2) Feb 2, 1773 Thomas Peterson received 100 acres in S.C. (Source) Manuscript in Edgefield Genealogy Society.
2 CONT
2 CONT (3) January 23, 1775 Thomas Peterson memorial 150 acres Chester Co., SC. On Cane Creek.
2 CONT
2 CONT (4) 1779 stub indent for flour N 540 Lib & "Issued the 13th ..July 1785 to Thomas Peterson for one pound eight Shillings and five pense 3/4 fo r 200 wt. ...flour Supply. d Militia in 1779 per account audited Principal...(source) B
2 CONC ook UW Stub Entries to Indents issued in Payment of Claims against SC Growing out of the Revolution. A.S. Salley, Jr. 1918.
2 CONT
2 CONT (5) April 25 - June 24 1781 Thomas Peterson served as a private in the British Army in the American Revolution. He served in Capt. Samuel Rowarth's Co., King's Rangers. He signed up in Augusta, GA (source) Vol 1, page 73 Loyalists
2 CONC  in the Southern Campaign of the Revolutionary War by Clark 1981.
2 CONT
2 CONT (6) On Feb 4, 1782, Thomas Peterson had land north of John Ferguson in Chester Co., SC. (source) Some SC Co. records Vol 2 by S.E. Lucas 1989, Chester Co., deed book C.
2 CONT
2 CONT (7) November 11, 1784 Thomas Peterson buys land from John Newton whose land was next to his in (On Little River) Newberry Co., SC. Paid off May 16, 1795, Newberry Co. Land Deeds Book "C", page 255.
2 CONT
2 CONT (8) June 2, 1788 Thomas Peterson buys land from William Johnson in Newberry Co., SC in Book "B", page 841.
2 CONT
2 CONT (9) Sept - Dec 1789, Thomas Peterson was on the Grand Jury in Newberry Co ., SC
2 CONT
2 CONT (10) Nov 8, 1813 Thomas Peterson sells land to Andrew Holand. Newberry Co. Land Deeds, book "K" pg. 310.
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
