1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Thomas was a soldier in the War of 1812.  He enlisted in Newberry District on 15 Sep 1814.  He appears on a Company Muster Roll for 6 Nov to 20 Dec 1814.  Date of joining rendezvous was 6 Nov 1814.  Term of service was 6 months.  
2 CONC He was a private in Capt. John K Griffin's Co. of Infantry, Regiment South Carolina Militia, commanded by Lt. Col. John A. Alston.  Discharged 1 Feb. 1815, in Georgetown, South Carolina. Soon after his discharge, Thomas began trav
2 CONC elling west.  His travels took him through Georgia, where, on 3 Nov 1817, he married Susannah Shaddock.  They were married by Thomas Monk, a Justice of the Peace, in Jasper County. Thomas and Susannah, continuing west, resided in 
2 CONC Bibb County, Alabama, in 1818, in Perry County, Alabama in 1822, and finally in Coosa County, Alabama in 1837. He was a farmer and wheelwright.  Both he and Susannah were members of the Primitive Baptist Church. The 1850, Alabama 
2 CONC Census Record, shows Susannah still living in Coosa County.  It also listed a Joseph Forbus, age 12, born in South Carolina, with Susannah (he was probably just visiting as there was a Forbus family next door?). In 1852, Susannah 
2 CONC is recorded as making application for bounty land, under the act passed 28 Sep 1850, while still residing in Coosa County.  Witnesses were James Goggans and Leonard Crawford.  She obtained a Land Warrant for eighty acres which she
2 CONC  disposed of. In 1856, she again made a declaration for the purpose of obtaining the bounty land to which she may be entitled under the act approved 3 Mar 1855.  Witnesses were William Chapman and Albert Thomas.
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
