1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 1750, William and Elizabeth purchased three hundred acres of land in Culpepper Co. Virginia. Here is a Copy of the land transaction; though lengthy, it proves interesting [or amusing] reading.    This Indenture mad
2 CONC e this Eighteenth day of October in the year of our Sovereign Lord George the Second by the Grace of God of Great Britain, France and Ireland King Defender of the Faith and in the year of our Lord God One Thousand Seven hundred an
2 CONC d Fifty, between Robert Coleman of the Parish of St. Mark and Culpepper County and Sarah his wife of the one part and William Goggan of the Parish of Lunenburgh and County of Richmond of the other part witnesseth that the said Rob
2 CONC ert Coleman and Sarah his wife for and in consideration of the sum of Twenty five Pounds current money of Virginia to them in hand paid by the acknowledged William Goggan and receipt whereof they therefrom doth fully, clearly and 
2 CONC absolutely acquit and discharge the aforesaid William Goggan and his heirs, Executors, Administrators and assigns and every of them by these present hath given, granted, bargained, sold, alien and enfeoff release and confirm unto 
2 CONC the said William Goggan his heirs, Executors, Administrators and assigns forever all that tract or parcel of land containing three hundred acres lying and being in the parish of St. Thomas and Culpepper County aforesaid which said
2 CONC  land was patented by Robert Coleman and bought of the said Robert Coleman by said Daniel Brown and is part of apatent granted by the said Coleman bearing Date the Twenty-eighth of September One Thousand Seven hundred and Thirty T
2 CONC wo and is bounded by the line of Joseph Shall (who holds one hundred acres out of the said patent) and lines of the land held by Daniel Brown now by Roland Cournelous and the Rev. John Thompson’ land which he bought of Samuel Ferg
2 CONC uson and according to the bounds of this patent not before conveyed to the said Shell together with all houses, out houses, gardens, orchards, fenced waters, water course ways, trees, woods, underwoods, privileges, liberties, prop
2 CONC erties, advantages and hereditaments whatsoever to the said tract or parcel of land or any part or parcel thereof in anyway belonging or appertaining and the  revision and revisions remainder or remainders thereof and of every par
2 CONC t and parcel thereof and all and singular the Estate Right, Title Interest, property claim and domains whatever that the said Robert Coleman or Sarah his wife hath or ought to have of, in, or the said three hundred acres of land o
2 CONC r any part thereof to have and to hold…[had enough?]  or  Cancel 
2 CONT
2 CONT Milo de Cogan to William and James Goggans PinedaleConnectionsadded this on 11 Oct 2009Much of the Goggans history remains obscure. The earliest known Goggans ancestor was Milo de Cogan who distinguished himself as a leader in the
2 CONC  Angle-Norman invasion of Ireland in 1171. During the centuries prior to the arrival of the Gogganses to the American Colonies the name underwent many changes resulting in numerous variations, namely: de Cogan, de Cogaine, O Cuaga
2 CONC in, MacCoggin, Cogan, Gogan, Coggin, Goggin, Goggan and many others with as many variations in spelling. The name Cogan was the Anglicized version of the Gaelic name “Cogagh.” Anglicized-To make English or similar to English in fo
2 CONC rm, idiom, style, or character: Gaelic-any of several related languages of the Celts-Pronounced: Kelts [unless, of course, you’re from Boston!] Celts-One of an Indo-European people–tall, fair-skinned wanderers originally of centra
2 CONC l Europe and spreading to Western Europe, the British Isles, Ireland and Scotland and southeast as Galatians. The Galatians were in their original part of that great Celtic migration which invaded Macedonia about B.C. 280.They wer
2 CONC e invited by the king of Bithynia to cross over into Asia Minor to assist him in his wars. There they ultimately settled, and being strengthened by fresh accessions of the same clan from Europe, they overran Bithynia, and supporte
2 CONC d themselves by plundering neighboring countries. They were great warriors, and hired themselves out as mercenary soldiers, sometimes fighting on both sides in the great battles of the times. They were at length brought under the 
2 CONC power of Rome in B.C. 189, and Galatia became a Roman province B.C. 25. This province of Galatia, within the limits of which these Celtic tribes were confined, was the central region of Asia Minor. During his second missionary jou
2 CONC rney Paul, accompanied by Silas and Timothy (Acts 16:6), visited the "region of Galatia," where he was detained by sickness (Gal. 4:13), and had thus the longer opportunity of preaching to them the gospel. On his third journey he 
2 CONC went over "all the country of Galatia and Phrygia in order" (Acts 18:23). Crescens was sent thither by Paul toward the close of his life (2 Tim. 4:10).] “Cogagh” means contention or strife. DeCogan means Son of Contention. Since t
2 CONC he name was Gaelic in origin it follows that the earliest Goggans ancestors may have come form the ancient land of Gaul (Normandy, France) having arrived in Briton and Ireland during the Norman Conquest which began in 1066. In 115
2 CONC 4 the Pope gave King Henry II of England permission to invade Ireland and bring the Irish church into line. Henry did not act on this until 1165 when Dermot Mac Murrough, King of Leinster sought his help. Dermot had been expelled 
2 CONC from Ireland after he lost out in a typical Irish dynastic struggle. Dermot had lost a war with Rory O'Connor, King of Connaught and High King of Ireland and his ally Tiernan O'Rourke, King of Breffni (modern Cavan and Leitrim). H
2 CONC enry gave Dermot permission to recruit Norman soldiers to help him regain his lands in Ireland. Dermot found the men he wanted on the marshes of south Wales, where the Barons were out of favor with Henry. Because they came from Wa
2 CONC les, many historians prefer to use the term Cambro-Norman rather than the more common Anglo-Norman, in the interests of accuracy. In May 1169 an advanced party of Normans under the command of Robert FitzStephen landed at Bannow Ba
2 CONC y, in Co Wexford. With their help Dermot quickly regained control of Leinster and captured the Viking town of Wexford. In August 1170 Richard FitzGilbert (known as Strongbow) arrived with a larger force and captured the Viking tow
2 CONC n of Waterford. Dermot's daughter Aoife married Strongbow. In September the Normans stormed Dublin; they then invaded Meath and attacked Breffni. In May 1171 Dermot died without a male heir, which left Strongbow as ruler of Leinst
2 CONC er. After the capture of Dublin King Henry, decided to curb the growing power of Strongbow and minimize the possibility that Strongbow might set-up an independent Kingdom in Ireland. In October 1171 he landed at Waterford. In retu
2 CONC rn for his submission Strongbow was granted Leinster. To act as a counterbalance Henry granted other parcels of land to the remainder of Strongbow's knights. Meath went to Hugh de Lacy and in Munster Milo de Cogan assumed lands to
2 CONC  the west of Cork harbor while Fitzstephen was granted lands to the east of Cork. Milo de Cogan (died circa 1183) was Strongbow's right-hand man in the Cambro-Norman invasion. He was granted a huge area in Co. Cork by Henry II and
2 CONC  the great territorial family was thus founded. Though reinforced by grants to Richard de Cogan in 1207 and still of sufficient importance to be listed among the chief gentry of the barony of Kinelea in 1591, as a family of power 
2 CONC it was practically extinct as such by the end of the seventeenth century. Minor branches of it, however, survive up to the present day, usually under the name of Goggin and sometimes Gogan. The original form of the name, de Cogan,
2 CONC  derives from Cogan, a parish in Cardiff. Patrick de Courcy, the second Baron of Kinsale, married the daughter of Miles De Cogan. Miles De Cogan ("the noblest baron in his time in Ireland" according to the Annals of the Four Maste
2 CONC rs) was killed in the battle of Athleathan, in 1316, in which Slemme De Exeter, lord of Athleathan who was in command of the English forces, was also slain. John de Courcy who succeeded as the 18th Baron of Kinsale on the decease 
2 CONC of his cousin, Gerald, the 17th Baron, marrried, first, Catherine, daughter of William Cogan William Cogan, M.P. for Cork County in 1585; and, secondly, Mary, daughter of Cornelius O'Cruly (or O'Crowley). As time passed, the Barre
2 CONC tts, Barrys, Roches and Fitzgeraids came to incur upon the holdings of the de Cogan family in Cork, as their fortunes declined. In 1405 it is given that the family was centred at Kerrycurrihy in Cork, and the name remained plentif
2 CONC ul here into the 17th century as evidenced by the census of 1659. Records indicate that the family was centred at Barnahely Castle and Goolmore Castle even after the decline of the family in the 15th century. They are additionally
2 CONC  tied to the castles of Aglish, Macroom, Crosshaven, Cullen, Ballyhea, Kilbolane and Rathgogan, among others. The Birth Index of 1890 gives the Cogan family centred in Co. Cork. Cogane is given as a principal name of Cork in 1659.
2 CONC  The family name has been traditionally linked to the district of Murragh in the Union of Bandon in Co. Cork. In the sixteenth century the name was in a transition stage: the earlier Fiants gave as a rule Cogan, Cogane and Coggain
2 CONC , the later ones Gogan and Goggan. Among the Co. Cork place-names in the same source we find Goganrath and Gogganshill, the latter being also given as Knockgogan and a few years earlier as Knockcowgan. The form Cogan, however, did
2 CONC  not become obsolete. Philip Cogan sailed to Spain with del Aquila in 1602; two Cogans and a Coggan were officers in southern regiments of James II's Irish army, Richard Cogan was a "doctor of physic" in Co. Cork in 1707 and in 17
2 CONC 98 Pascho Coggin was a witness to a deed relating to Charleville. Charleville, by the way, was formerly known as Rathgoggan - Rathgoggan, indeed, is the name of the civil parish embracing the town of Charleville. Another Philip Co
2 CONC gan (1750-1834), a composer of some note, was also a Cork man. A very valuable study of the various families of Cogan has been made by Mr. Dermot Murphy, which was presented at University College, Cork, in 1957 as a thesis. In add
2 CONC ition to the Cogans of Norman origin there is a set of the Ui Maine whose name is sometimes anglicized as Cogan, though Coogan is more usual and nearer the Gaelic-Irish form Ó Cuagáin. They are of the same stock as the O'Maddens. 
2 CONC The small sept of Mac Cogain (the Book of Fenagh and the Topographical Poem spelled it Mac Cagadhain), who were located in Glanfarne on the shore of Lough Allen in Co. Leitrim, dropped the prefix Mac in the eighteenth century and 
2 CONC became Cogan and Coggan. Neither of these, however, is at all numerous today. The variant Cogavin is also rare. Matheson's Synonymes, published in 1901, mentions Cogan and Keogan as synonymous in Co. Cavan; and I have noticed in a
2 CONC  Co. Meath will of 1834 the testator described as Cogan alias Keogan. Keogan, however, is properly quite a different name from those considered above. Woulfe, mentioning that it is Ó Ceogáin in the spoken Irish language, gives Mac
2 CONC  Eochagáin as the correct Irish form of it, which is a variant of the better known Mag Eochagáin (MacGeoghegan). Heraldry Cogan (The Anglo Norman family of de Cogan, descended from Milo or Myles de Cogan who accompanied Strongbow 
2 CONC to Ireland in the twelfth century and was granted lands in county Cork) From the time of Milo de Cogan who was granted land in the County Cork region of Southern Ireland by Henry the II of England, Grandson of William the Conquero
2 CONC r, history has recorded a succession of descendents. In the sixteen and seventeen hundreds the most common spelling was Cogan, Cogane, Coggin, Gogan and Goggin. The most common spelling variation in Ireland today is Goggin and Gog
2 CONC an. The most common variations in the United States today are Coggins, Goggins and Goggans. The Goggins and Goggans spelling is used interchangeably within the same family branch. The Coggins and Goggins have long since been separ
2 CONC ate branches of the original family tree. County (Corcaigh. From corcach, meaning "marsh") Cork is the largest county in Ireland, and its size has had an appreciable effect on the mentality of its inhabitants; they have been known
2 CONC  to refer to journeys to other parts of Ireland as "visiting the Republic". The county has an extraordinary variety of landscapes, from the lush lowlands and valleys of east and central Cork to the barren magnificence of the mount
2 CONC ains and peninsulas of west Cork. Cork city is the second largest in Ireland, though not in the minds of many Corkonians, and is beautifully situated at the mouth of the Lee valley. Before the advent of the Normans in the twelfth 
2 CONC century, the county was part of the kingdom of Desmond, ruled by the McCarthys, following their expulsion from Tipperary by their arch-enemies, the O'Briens. As English rule in Ireland became more secure through the sixteenth cent
2 CONC ury, large areas were granted to English "undertakers", who included Sir Walter Raleigh and the poet Edmund Spenser, but the rebellions and wars of the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries put paid to the scheme. The Goggins/Goggan
2 CONC s family, whose descendants migrated to the Pontotoc County Mississippi area around 1845, first appeared in the Richmond County area of Virginia in the early 1700s. The earliest known record of this Goggins branch in America dates
2 CONC  back to 1718, to a William and James Goggin. According to Transcripts of State Papers Relating to Ireland, William and James came to Virginia in 1718. 
2 CONT
