1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Biography==
2 CONT '''John Wesley Magnum, Jr.''' was born on January 19, 1763, in Lunenburg, Lunenburg County, Virginia, United States. His parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-33377427 John William Mangum, Sr.] and [https://www.geni.com/profi
2 CONC le-19161392 Mary Elizabeth Mangum]. He was a Revolutionary War Soldier. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John married the first wife, [https://www.geni.com/profile-62575596 Gemima Mangum] circa 1802 in Newberry, Newberry, South Carolina. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34656567521 Cyrus Mangum]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34724259621 Mary Mangum]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John married his 2nd wife, [https://www.geni.com/profile-33377814 Mary Mangum (Murdock)] in 1780 in Sc. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34724259617 James Mangum]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34724259616 Nancy Mangum].
2 CONT
2 CONT John married the 3rd time to, [https://www.geni.com/profile-19161376 Rebecca Canida Mangum] on January 19, 1809, in Lebano, Warren Co. Oh. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-19161361 Jemima Catherine Adair]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-83897734 William M. Mangum]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-62575272 Rebecca Mangum]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-33377393 John Mangum, III]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-62574648 James Mitchell Mangum]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-6815033 Joseph Eastland Mangum]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-62575044 Jane Mangum]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-62575105 Lucinda Richey]; 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34723362597 Elizabeth Peeks]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died on March 23, 1843, in Fulton, Itawamba, Mississippi, and was buried circa March 1843 there. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ----
2 CONT '''The Mangums'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Rev. War veteran and progenitor of the large group of Utah Mangum's. See Ivie Hale's book on the Utah Magnums for this complete line. Source: Brenda Moody, Mesa Arizona
2 CONT
2 CONT John is the great-grandson of John Mangum and Rebecca Bennet.  He served in the Rev War and has many descendants today.  Noteworthy is the fact that it was some of his children and his wife, Rebecca, who went through Nauvoo, Illin
2 CONC ois, on the trek to Salt Lake City, UT, after being converted to Mormonism by John Richie, a Mormon missionary, who was in Fulton, MS.  Rebecca later married Mr. Richie at Nauvoo and went with him to Utah  (See Delta Ivie Mang um 
2 CONC Hale's book written in 1971 on "John Mangum, American Revolutionary War Soldier, and his descendants.)
2 CONT
2 CONT She wrote "His parents apparently moved to Lunenburg County soon after he was born, as his father, John Mangum, is listed as a member of the St. James Parish, Lunenburg County in the year 1764, the year after John was born. As Joh
2 CONC n grew older, he was active in the Baptist Church, as he and his brother Lewis are both listed as members. (South Carolina Baptists, pp 165-66; Bush River Baptist Church Records.) In 1805 he moved from Newberry District, SC to War
2 CONC ren Co, afterward to C Clinton Co, Ohio.  In 1811 he moved to Giles Co, TN.  In 1815 he moved to St  Clair Co, AL, and in 1823/24 to Pickens Co, AL.  His post office address there was Carrollton, Pickens Co, AL.
2 CONT
2 CONT An interesting story has been handed down through the family, as told by Bishop Hugh Richey of St. Johns, Arizona, and was originally told to him by his father, James Moroni Richey.  "My great-grandfather enlisted in Marion's Brig
2 CONC ade at the age of 16 and served four years to the end of the war. Brig. Gen Francis Marion organized his brigade of frontiersmen who furnished their own fast horses, arms, and food and who could be armed and in the saddles in a ma
2 CONC tter of minutes upon call.  They would destroy the British supply trains cut off any small detachments, rush into their main camps at night for a raid and be in another county before dawn.  When the British would chase them with a
2 CONC  large force, they would hide in the swamps and mountains or scatter to their homes until the danger was past and then be at it again.  They were the best marksmen, riders, and woodsmen on the frontier. Francis Marion was called t
2 CONC he Swamp Fox.  At one time he was surprised while he and his men were all taking a bath in the river.  They all ran for their guns but did not have time to get their clothes.  He sent a flag of truce to demand their clothes or he 
2 CONC would kill ten of their best men.  Col, Sir Banstre Tarleton sent the clothes.  The British soldier who brought the clothes was asked to eat with Marion and his men.  Upon his return to his own lines, he told Tarleton that anyone 
2 CONC who could eat sweet potatoes like Marion's men would never surrender and that Marion had said, "Tell the Colonel Banstre Tarleton that I will only kill eight of his men now," which of course he didn't. (The Encyclopedia Americana,
2 CONC  1957 Edition, Vol 18, p. 292.)
2 CONT
2 CONT John had another brush with death.  While he was in the service, he was permitted to go home on a furlough.  When returning to the service, the crew was captured and taken prisoner by the British and kept for several days.  They w
2 CONC ere taken out to a log and had their heads laid on it and then chained to the log.  The commanding officer drew his sword and raked two or three of them across the head and told the Captain to turn them loose.  John Mangum was one
2 CONC  of the boys where was spared.  After a while, John had a chance to talk to the commanding officer and asked him why their lives were spared.  The officer swore and told him that he knew his brother, William, who was a Tory and th
2 CONC at he thought he would make a good one too. ! His service record indicates that John Mangum was in the following battles and battle areas: Augusta, GA; Cup Board Creek, GA; Keoka Creek, GA; Edgell's Old Field, SC where he was take
2 CONC n prisoner and received a wound on his head from William Cunningham, one of the Tories; Anderson's Mile in Edgefield eld District, SC; Bacon's Bridge near Charleston, SC. John is listed in "MS Rev Soldiers," published by the DAR, 
2 CONC p 201-202.  references listed at the end of the article on p 202 are: Albemarle Parish, VA Records Pension Records ALA Records, Jones & Gandrud 1840 List of Miss. Pensioners DAR Line accepted 1951 Research: Mrs. C.L. Willardson, S
2 CONC an Diego, CAL
2 CONT
