2 CONT
2 CONT The Mangum cemetery is located on Highway 56, 8 miles NW of Silverstreet. The headstone of William Mangum in the Mangum Family Cemetery on the Mangum Plantation in Newberry Co., SC reads 'In memory of William Mangum, Sr. who depar
2 CONC ted this life the 5th of July 1827 in the 72nd year of his age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cemetery notes and/or description:Mangum Family Cemetery is located on a ridge to the left of the "Granny Mangum Spring" and original Mangum homesite on the original Mangum Plantation at the junction of the Newberry County Highway
2 CONC  #58 (Old Belefast Road) and Highway #22. The original road was called the "King's Road" - Indian Trail.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8 miles northwest of Silverstreet.5 Interments: Anna Goggans Mangum 20 May 1756 d. 30 Apr 1848 Isabella McKittrick Mangum b.1800 d. 13 Jul 1823 William Mangum b. 12 Nov 1756 d. 5 Jul 1827 Nancy Mangum Peterson b. 30 Aug 1786 d. 11
2 CONC  Jan 1869 William Peterson b. 11 Apr 1780 d. 22 Oct 1866
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Delta Ivie Mangum Hale's "JOHN MANGUM, AMERICAN REVOLUTIONAR Y WAR SOLDIER AND HIS DESCENDANTS", 1971. Descendants of John Mangum, the Rev. War Soldier, relate a story in which John was captured by the Tories during the wa
2 CONC r but was saved from being killed by his captors since the Captain knew John's brother, William, who was a Tory. (See page 169 of John Palmer's family history, "THE PALMERS AND PARMERS OF NC, AL, AND MS", 1986, for an account of p
2 CONC erhaps another William Mangum b 16 May 1736, who also served with the British.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. William may have been involved with his father's estate. Colleen Gwynn notes an Oct. 1797 term in Newberry Co., South Carolina. Colleen in her research notes states that William Mangum is the son and heir of John Mangum making 
2 CONC him a sibling to John Mangum the patriot who is his next door neighbor in the 1790 census in Newberry Co., SC. She also indicates William and his wife Anna are buried in the Mangum Family Graveyard in Silverstreet near Bush River,
2 CONC  South Carolina. Bush River is located near the l and noted in John Mangum's [the patriot] research notes in this database.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. The book "John Mangum, American Revolutionary War Soldier and Descendents," 1986, p. 7-16, by Delta Ivie Mangum Hale with notes in [ ] by myself, Kerry Petersen: "John Mangum [sibling of this individual] was born Jan . 19, 1763
2 CONC  in Mecklenburg County, Virginia. His parents apparently moved to Lunenburg County soon after he was born, as his father, John Mangum, is listed as a member of the St. James Parish, Lunenburg County in the year 1764, the year afte
2 CONC r John was born. He was the fourth child in a family of six. He had one brother, William, and two sisters, Lucy and Sarah who were older and two brothers William and Lewis who were younger. (The reader may question the fact that t
2 CONC he first and fifth children were both named William. It was a practice, especially in England, that when a child died, the next child of that sex would receive the dead child's name. )...
2 CONT
2 CONT [Some authors purport that there was only one William child in this family -- John Palmer as cited below notes that the headstone of William Mangum in the Mangum Family Cemetery on the Mangum Plantation in Newberr y Co., SC reads 
2 CONC 'In memory of William Mangum, Sr. who departed this life the 5th of July 1827 in the 72nd year of his age...' thus establishing a birth date of 1756.] John's father, whose name is also John, was born about 1736 in Albemarle Parish
2 CONC , Surry County, Virginia. His mother's name was Mary. Her maiden name is unknown. His grandfather, William Mangum, was also born in Albemarle Parish, Surry County, Virginia. His grandmother was Mary Person Mangum. [William and Mar
2 CONC y Person Mangum as grandparents is since disputed.] His great-grandfather was John Mangum, and his great- grandmother was Francis Bennett Mangum, daughter of Governor Richard Bennett of Virginia. This is as far back as the direct 
2 CONC Mangum line in America has been determined at present." [Kerry's note: The ancestor in terms of Governor Richard Bennett is erroneous in my opinion. See notes for Thomas Bennett, Richard Bennett, Sr., and Richard Bennett, Jr. sepa
2 CONC rately in this database for a detailed Bennett history. Even though both groups of Bennetts lived in Isle of Wight County in colonial Virginia, there does not appear to be a connection and both Richards are separate and distinct.]
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Censuses: 1790 US: The following Mangum, Richey, Goggins, and Murdock names occur in Newberry Counties, South Carolina -- none with that surname appear in neighboring Laurens county where the Adairs were located: P. 57, William
2 CONC  Murdock: males over 16: 1; males under 16: 1; females: 1 ; slaves: 0. P. 74, Ambrose Hudgins, 1-1-5-0 (Possible father to Austen Hudgens who married Elizabeth Mangum? On the previous p. 72 [73 is blank], there is a Sam'l Hugghen 
2 CONC who could also be a possibility but probably not. In neighboring Laurens Co., there are also two other "Ambros" Hudgins: pp. 433 an d 443 with most Adairs in that county occuring on pp. 440-442. There are various Huggins, Hudgens,
2 CONC  Hudggens, Huggans in South Carolina -- but with none in Newberry or Laurens except as noted above.) P. 74, James Goggin, 2-4-5-2. Separated from Ambrose above by 10 names. P. 74, Geo. Goggens, 2-3-2-0. (Separated from James above
2 CONC  by 26 names.) P. 74, William Goggins, 1-3-3-0. (Separated from Geo. above by 6 names.) P. 76 (note page 75 is blank), John Mangum, 1-0-1-0. (Separated from William Goggins above by 58 names.) P. 76 (note page 75 is blank), Wm. Go
2 CONC ggins, 2-2-1-0. (Separated from John Mangum above by 0 names.) P. 76 (note page 75 is blank), Wm. Mangum, 2-2-2-0. (Separated from Wm. Goggins by 2 names.) P. 76 (note page 75 is blank), Robert Richey, 1-1-6-1. (Separated from Wm 
2 CONC . Mangum by 39 names.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Probable estate of John Mangum per FHL book 975.739 P2b, Vol. 1 "Newberry County South Carolina Probate Estate Abstracts"; William is most like ly his son: "Box 357, Pkg. 32 (357-32), 1794, John Mangum. William Mangum, admin. C
2 CONC it . No Date By Daniel Williams -to appear 3rd Monday in Oct Next 1794 for Letters of Administration. -read 12 Oct 1794 by Jesse Brooks; read 19 Oct 1794 at Little River Meeting House by Daniel M---gum?; read 31 Jul 1794 by Daniel
2 CONC  Towles. Let. 20 Oct 1794 To William Mangum as Adminstator. Bk. A, p. 252. A.B. 20 Oct 1794 50 pounds. - Wm. Mangum, Daniel McKie, Stephen Waldrop. W.A. 20 Oct 1794; recorded Bk. A, p. 252 -James Waldrop, Charles Griffin, John Flo
2 CONC yd, Daniel McKie. App. 16 Nov 1794; recorded Bk. A, p. 252. Total 2:9:6 -books, bed, shotgun, pewter, 100 acres of land. Sale - 10 Jun 1795; recorded Bk A. p. 279. Total 7:19:4 -Wm. Mangum and Richard Waldrop; said Mangum bought t
2 CONC he 100 acres of land ."
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. William was probably the father of Reverend Daniel Mangum, 24 May 1788 - 25 Sep 1852. Note: Pastor for 26 years at Bush River Baptist Church, Newberry, South Carolina and other associated Baptist churches. Died at age 64. Burie
2 CONC d in Bush River Church Cemetery. References FHL book 975.731 H 2b â[euro]œA Laurens County Sketchbook,â[euro][??] by Julian Stevenson Bolick include: Pg. 54: "The Hurricane Baptist Church is nestled amid the pines bordering Hurric
2 CONC ane Road near Hurricane Creek in Hunter Township. It was establised as a mission church in August, 1832, through the efforts of the elders from the Bush River Church in Newberry County; thus, it has served the community for 140 ye
2 CONC ars. Records show that the same leaders -- Daniel Mangum, James Harlan and Elbert Lindsay -- were active in both the Bush River and Hurricane churches. Mr. Lindsay served as the first pastor of the mission church."
2 CONT
2 CONT Pg. 55: "Mount Pleasant Baptist. Bethabara Baptist Church minutes of June 10, 1826, show that application had been made by members to withdraw and form a constititued 'arm' church at Cole's Meetinghouse. With permission granted, M
2 CONC ount Pleasant Baptist Church in the Waterloo Township became an established place for worship August 19, 1826. Elders Henry Hitt and Daniel Mangum (the former, one of the organizers) appear to have been the first two pastors to se
2 CONC rve. For ninety years the church had no baptistry, and the waters of nearby Walnut Creek served for baptismal services . The 'old pewter set' -- pitcher plates and goblets bought for the communion table in 1856 -- may be seen toda
2 CONC y in a glass case in the sanctuary."
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: 1. Land transaction records info per the book "John Mangum, American Revolutionary War Soldier and Descendents," 1986, pp. 15-16, by Delta Ivie Ma ngum Hale: "Although only part of the record of John's land holdings and
2 CONC  transactions is availble, it is evident that he had possession of a great deal of land during his lifetime. The following is a record of some of these transactions. 'John Mangum, brother of William, and wife, Mary (Murdock) conve
2 CONC yed 37 acres of land in 1803 in Newberry County, South Carolina, founded a part of original Surrey County and other lands. Again in 1805, John Mangum sold 165 acres of land to Thomas Atkinson. John Mangum bought 100 acres of land 
2 CONC from Joseph Blacksmith Aug. 7, 1779 on Little River, now Newberry County, South Carolina, being one half of 200 acres granted to Elizabeth Johnson in 1772 and conveyed by her to Joseph Hayes in 1773. This land is located on the si
2 CONC de of the main road from Rayburn's Creek which is the dividing line of the 200 acres.' [Source: Columbia, So. Carolina Court file #4754 of John Mangum before and since the fall of Charleston.] It is interesting to note the price o
2 CONC f land at the beginning of the nineteenth century. The following is a recorded land transaction of his brother, William. 'William Mangum, brother of John Mangum, bought from William Johnson a tract of 84 acres for $150 on Jan. 6, 
2 CONC 1805. The same being part of an original grant of Jacob Johnson and situated on Little River and bounded by Charles Pitts on the Southwest and all other sides by William Peterson and Daniel Mangum.' [Source: Deed Book G in the Off
2 CONC ice of the Clerk of the Court, Newberry, South Carolina, p. 264.] The following information was received from Enid B. Willardson of National City , California: 'In later years following the death of his brother, William , John ass
2 CONC isted his brother's widow in the settling of William's Estate . Anna Mangum, William's widow, relinguished the adminstration of her husband's Estate to John Mangum on July 7, 1827. He settled the Estate on Aug. 17, 1831.' The foll
2 CONC owing land transaction with his brother's widow is also recorded: 'Signed in the presence of David Carson and Howard Peterson, recorded on Jan. 19, 1838. Anna Mangum of Newberry District sold to John Mangum 82 acres for $7.75. Bou
2 CONC nded by Carson's Creek on the south side of the road from Belfast to Newberry, So. Carolina. Delivered Oct . 8, 1828.' [Source: Deed Book in the Office of the Clerk of the Court, Newberry, South Carolina.]" MARRIAGE: 1. Probable s
2 CONC isters Gemima and Anna Goggins married brothers William and John Mangum. Some say the Goggins father was Hamilton Goggins; however , I see no mention of that name in censuses. However in the land records and censuses in close prox
2 CONC imity to John Mangum are several Goggans. ORDINANCES: BAPTISM: ENDOWMENT: SEALING TO PARENTS: SEALING TO SPOUSE: SOURCES_MISC: 1. From family group sheet submitted by Delta I.M. Hale, Rt. 1, Blackfoot , Idaho. She references corre
2 CONC spondence from Surry Co., Virginia; from J.P. Madsen, Richmond, Virginia (researcher); Albemarle Co. Parish records Va A2, p;04,118,1514, Va. Mag. of Hist. and Biog. July 1894, p. 108. 2. FHL film 2056023-2056026, especially film 
2 CONC 5 which contains Mangum-Adair materials. Title is "George Addison Mangum's Genealogical Collection" which is his lifetime work donated to library in 1998; he was born in Utah in 1922, of Blackfoot, ID and is perhaps a brother to I
2 CONC vey Mangum Hale . Brief summary includes: 2056023, item 1, book of remembrance; item 2, surname index of changes and volumes; item 3, Person materials; 2056024, items 3 thru 6 and 2056026, Mangum/Adair materials. Included are many
2 CONC  family group sheets of sidelines and downlines, many of them unconnected.
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
