3 STAE Missouri
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 1.  Also in household in 1850 Scott Co. Census is Joseph Ulmer, age 21, born SC.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  Thomas is listed as being the Postmaster for the area during 1862-65.  (Nodaway Co., Missouri)
2 CONT
2 CONT On 21 Mar 1839 when Thomas G was 22, he married Sara McCullough, 970, F, daughter of George McCullough, 954, M (17 Jun 1780-25 Sep 1867) & Jennet Thompson, 955, F (5 Aug 1785-13 Mar 1859), in Scott Co., IN. Born on 13 Sep 1819 in 
2 CONC Chester Co., SC. Sara died in Hopkins, MO, on 31 Oct 1907; she was 88.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  Info from Lynne Emmons
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  Moved to Nodaway Co., MO along with sister Elizabeth & family
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.  Info from Tom Kephart, she was also known as Sally
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.  Scott Co. marriage book A, p. 158/159
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.  Ron Ulmer - 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas and Sarah moved to Nodaway Co., Missouri about 1859. They settled in the area that was called White Cloud. White Cloud was located about 6.5 miles north of Maryville and about 1 to 1.5 miles northwest of Pickering, Mo. Thes
2 CONC e were pioneer days and the times were still very hard for those who settled in this part of the state. The county was formed in 1845, only about 14 years prior to Thomas and Sarah moving there. Many of the people in the area live
2 CONC d in small log cabins. Some no bigger than a 8X10 or 10X12 foot structure. Some had no windows and only a blanket to cover the door entry.
