1 NAME Henry /Zorn/ Sr.
2 GIVN Henry
2 SURN Zorn
2 NSFX Sr.
2 _MARNM Zorn
2 NICK John Henry Zorn
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE BET 1735 AND 1739
2 ADDR
3 STAE Province of South Carolina
3 CTRY Colonial America
1 BURI
2 DATE 1800
2 PLAC Barnwell, Barnwell, South Carolina, United States
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 22 AUG 1800
2 ADDR
3 STAE South Carolina
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} DAR Ancestor # [https://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A201222 A201222] Patriotic Service 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT (John) Henry Zorn, was born in Orangeburg, South Carolina, a son of Nicholas Zorn, who immigrated from Switzerland and his wife, Anna Catherine. Henry died August 22, 1800 at the age of 64-65 years, in Barnwell, South Carolina. [4
2 CONC ]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT Upson-Talbot County GaArchives Biographies.....Zorn, James December 28 1807 - December 18 1881 < [http://files.usgwarchives.net/ga/upson/bios/gbs27zorn.txt link] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT HENRY ZORN, SR.  b. abt 1740 in SC d. 1800 in SC: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Married:  ELIZABETH (Maiden name unknown) b. 1746 in Orangeburg District  SC d. after 1807
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of Elizabeth (unknown) and Henry Zorn, Sr.:
2 CONT *1.  '''Ann Zorn''' b. 1760  Barnwell, SC m. John Henry Hutto  b.5/26/1853 d. 08/27/1817 Orangeburg, SC;Parents, John & Ann Tushay; children: Rachel, Wm, Nicholas, Isaac, and Rebecca
2 CONT *2.	David Zorn b. 1768 in Orangeburg SC; m. Nancy ?; children: Rebecca &  David; Rebecca m. Frances Hartzog, 1843 b. 1824 SC d. 10/20/1877Ala. Children: Sarah, George, Jane,Daniel, Mosley, Henry and James.
2 CONT * 3.	Catherine Zorn b. 1770 Orangeburg SC; m. William Hightower in SC
2 CONT * 4.	Susanna b. 1774 Orangeburg District SC; m. Daniel Hartzog in SC, parents John and Ann Felder Hartzog; children: Elizabeth, Rebecca, Sarah,  Susannah, Georgeand Daniel Charles.
2 CONT * 5.	Rebecca Zorn b. 1778 Orangeburg District SC; m. Charly Mathany.
2 CONT * 6.	NICHOLAS ZORN III b.1780 Orangeburg SC; m. Susannah Elizabeth ?;
2 CONT * 7.	Elizabeth b. 1783 Orangeburg, SC; m. John F. Mitchell in SC. ALSO
2 CONT * 8. Henry Zorn b. 1782 Orangeburg District SC m. Ann 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Zorn-161
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Zorn performed Patriotic Service in South Carolina in the American Revolution.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Zorn was born circa 1740 and was living in the Orangeburg District of South Carolina, at the time of the American Revolutionary War, where he furnished supplies in support of the Continental Army. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry is honored for his Patriotic Service by the Society of the Daughters of the American Revolution as DAR Ancestor #A201222. Henry furnished supplies to the Continental Army. DAR records indicate that Henry died before August 2
2 CONC 2, 1800, in the Barnwell District of South Carolina, created in 1797 (effective in 1800) from the southwestern portion of the Orangeburg District, along the Savannah River. Applications for membership in the Society of the DAR hav
2 CONC e been made by the descendants of his son, '''Henry Zorn, Jr.''' whose wife's name was Ann. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Zorn and Nicholas Zorn are found on the United States Census of 1800, living in the town of Orange, in the Orangeburg District of South Carolina. [3] An image of the Census may be viewed on FamilySearch.org here: https://www
2 CONC .familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:33S7-9RZ8-HF?i=3&cc=1804228&personaUrl=%2Fark%3A%2F61903%2F1%3A1%3AXH5R-ZF3
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Notes 
2 CONT
2 CONT From: First Families of Orangeburgh District, South Carolina, digitized here: https://ogsgs.org/ffam/ffam.php?f=zorn
2 CONT
2 CONT "HENRY ZORN, SR. was born abt. 1735-39 and died in 1800. He married ELIZABETH UNKNOWN. Henry surveyed a grant of 200 acres on 2 Oct 1772[10] and it was granted on 2 Apr 1773[2]. In addition he purchased a 640 acre tract on the Sal
2 CONC kehatchie from James McMillan, who had a plat for this land recorded on 22 Sept 1784[16]. Henry was a petit juror "for Orangeburgh" (meaning he lived in or near the town) in 1778-9[5]. The 1790 census shows Henry's family breakdow
2 CONC n as 3 4 4 0 7, showing he had a large family and owned seven slaves. Henry died in 1800 and there was a partition deed (A: 50) recorded in Barnwell Co. in which his children and spouses divided up the tract that Henry had bought 
2 CONC from James McMillan. They sold to Nicholas and David Zorn. In 1809, another deed (F-165) deals with the estate of Henry Zorn Sr.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From The Zorns in America (continued): http://files.usgwarchives.net/ga/upson/bios/gbs27zorn.txt
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas and Cathrina entered America via the Charleston Harbor, SC in 1735.  They left behind the ZornCastle in the Rhine Valley and members of the Zorn family.  Not far from the castle is the Zorn River by which there is held Ro
2 CONC se  Festivals*.Little did they know that two hundred years later Zorns would be  growing Roses in Tyler, Smith Co. Texas or that a beautiful Rose Festival would be held there annually.  
2 CONT
2 CONT After entering the Charleston Harbor in South Carolina  the Zorns settled in the Orangeburg District of the state.  That District was made up of the present counties of Orangeburg, Barnwell, and Bamberg.  They were given land gran
2 CONC ts on South Edisto River near Bamberg, SC and on Little 
2 CONT Salkehatchie River In the area of Denmark-Govan, SC. Records in the present day Barnwell County Courthouse show the River as the Little “Saltkatcher” and present day residents still refer to it by that name. Some of the Zorns stil
2 CONC l live in this area. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The  grants on the Edisto and Salkehatchie Rivers were given to '''Henry Zorn''' for furnishing provisions and forage to the Continental States Troops and Militia-Revolutionary War.  Henry  Zorn is also shown on Roster of Company 
2 CONC of Captain Lewis Golson of Colonel John Chevellett’s Regiment, Cherokee Way 1759-1762.
2 CONT
2 CONT The records in the Barnwell County, SC Courthouse state that two brothers, Nicholas and David Zorn bought 300 acres “on the Bull Pond” in the District of Barnwell “on the waters of the Little Saltkatcher River (from their father’s
2 CONC  estate) being part of an original land grant to Henry Zorn containing 640 acres” And that they also purchases a 54 acre tract adjoining the 300 acres form heirs of their father.
2 CONT
