2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me}     from file of Kimberly Raimey:The surname "Dentzler" and its other variations in phonetic spelling apparently originated in what is now northeastern Switzerland. The name Denzler apparently arose from leadership
2 CONC  or participation in the medieval folk dances which were important prt of European culture. by the med-1400's there were records of the Denzler family in the village of Nanikon, just north of Greiffensee, a lake east-southeast of 
2 CONC the city of Zurich, in the Canton of Zurich. The Denzler family apparently remained prominent in the affiars to Nanikon through the centuries and some members moved to nearby villages.
2 CONT
2 CONT     The earliest known direct Denzler ancestors of the families who emigrated to S C and GA lived in the little village of Hermikon, about 3 miles from Nanikon. thomas Denzler and his wife Elsbetha Winkler, both born about 1590, h
2 CONC ad a family consisting of seven daughters and one son, Heinrich, their youngest child, was born in 1632. The father, Thomas, died when Heinrich was very young. Heinrich married Regula Bachofner by 1659 and they had at least five c
2 CONC hildren, including three sons: Jacob (born 1670), Hans Jacob (born 1674) and Heinrich (born 1678).
2 CONT
2 CONT     In 1697, Heinrich Denzler of Hermikon purchased a farm in the village of First, a little hamlet located on a gentle hilltop about six miles northeast of Hermikon. He moved to First with his family, which included his three you
2 CONC ng adult sons, Jacob, Hans Jacob and Heinrich Denzler. The elder Heinrich died on 5 May 1700. Young Heinrich on 10 January 1699 married Barbara Brungger of first, who had been baptized 1 January 1671. Barbara's ancestry can be tra
2 CONC ced in the records of Illnau and adjacent parishes to the families of Brungger of Agasul, Stephan of Moosburg, Winkler of Theilingen, Riesh of Kloten, Moroff of Effretikon, Houser of Fehraltdorf, Brungger of Billikon, Stadeli of N
2 CONC urensdorf, and Rudiman. In most instances, these lines of family descent can be followed back to the late 1500's.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Heinrich Denzler and his wife Barbara had eleven children whose baptisms are recorded, four of who died in early childhood. Their first son was Conrad, who was baptised 4 March 1703, and their second was Hans Ulrich, baptised 
2 CONC 3 August 1704. These two sons were the brothers who, with their families, emigrated to Georgia and South Carolina respectively.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Conrad Denzler married on 6 May 1723 Anna Wezstein of Kemleten, who bore him seven children baptized i Illnau Parish. Of these five survived to leave Switzerland with their parents in 1737, in the company of Hans Wezstein and 
2 CONC his wife Anna Frauenfelder with their children. Hans Wezstein and his family made their way to Orangeburg, S C, but Conrad Denzler and his family arrived in Savannah, Georgia, on 20 December 1737. In late 1742, Conrad was granted 
2 CONC land in the settlement of Vernonberg (White Bluff) near Savannah. conrad died in 1749, leaving a widow and several children. In this Georgia branch of the family, the name Denzler came to be spelled Densler. Henry Densler and his 
2 CONC children remained in Gerogia and should not be confused with those of similar names in South Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Title: Kimberly Raimey <draimey@aol.com> Rootsweb Submitter 
