1 NOTE {geni:about_me} To the Memory of Rachel Smoke, Widow of the Rev. Jacob Wannamaker and Daughter of the Rev. Wm. Whetstone. Who Departed this life on the morning of the 28th of September 1856, Aged 56 Years and 4 Days.* Reference: [
2 CONC https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/120605828/rachel-smoke-whetstone Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Mar 4 2020, 0:46:53 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/120605828/rachel-smoke-whetstone
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/9NCX-QVQ
2 CONT
2 CONT Rachel Whetstone was born on 1799 Sep 24 in South Carolina's Orangeburg District to William Whetstone & Margaret Zimmerman. 
2 CONT
2 CONT South Carolina, in 1800, split Orangeburg District into both Orangeburg County and Barnwell County, and in 1804 split Orangeburg County to create Lexington County, but Rachel remained within Orangeburg County.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Rachel was married about 1817 in South Carolina's Orangeburg County to Jacob Wannamaker, who was a widower of Mary Magdalena Miller. Rachel & Jacob resided in South Carolina's Orangeburg County. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rachel helped raise her 6 new stepchildren and bore at least 7 children of her own, namely Rachel, John, Jacob, Asbury, Marcus, Thomas, and Hayne. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rachel was widowed in 1834. Rachel was remarried on 1838 Nov 25 in South Carolina's Orangeburg County at Saint Matthews to Adam Smoke. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rachel & Adam resided in South Carolina's Orangeburg County. Rachel worked as a homemaker and bore one more child---a daughter named Amanda. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rachel died on 1856 Sep 28 in South Carolina's Orangeburg County, and was buried afterward there in Wannamaker Family Cemetery with her husband. South Carolina in 1908 split Orangeburg County to create Calhoun County, which includ
2 CONC ed Rachel's gravesite.
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/120605828/rachel-smoke-whetstone Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''May 22 2022, 0:52:10 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Residence''': Orangeburg, South Carolina, United States - 12 November 1850
