1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Non Quincy Adams - a longtime businessman and resident of Mobile, passed away at his home on Tuesday, October 9, in the loving company of his family. He is survived by his wife Eran Jobe Adams, brother Charles Adam
2 CONC s of Mobile, sister Dora Lee Davidson of Montgomery, daughter Laura Aline Adams, son Samuel Russell Adams, granddaughter Laura Eran Hanenkrat and great-grandson Damien Hanenkrat, all of Mobile. A Mobile native, N.Q. Adams led a di
2 CONC stinguished career as President and CEO of First National Bank and AmSouth Bank. The son of lumberman S. B. Adams, he was born on June 1, 1925, grew up on South Georgia Avenue and attended Barton Academy, Leinkauf and Murphy High 
2 CONC School. After attending Millsaps College and serving in the Navy during World War II, he earned his business and law degrees from the University of Alabama, then joined First National Bank's Trust Department in 1951. He was an act
2 CONC ive member of Keep Mobile Beautiful, the Camellia Society of Mobile, United Way, the Mobile Chamber of Commerce, the Red Cross, the Boy Scouts of America, Exploreum, the Mobile Community Foundation, Delta Kappa Epsilon, the Busine
2 CONC ss Council of Alabama, the Athelstan Club, various mystic societies and numerous other civic organizations. He served on the boards of T. R. Miller Lumber Company, Turner Supply Company, Alco Land and Timber, Loyal American Life, 
2 CONC the W. T. Neal Trust and AmSouth. While serving on Mobile's School Board, he was named Mobilian of the Year by the Civitan Club in 1991. One of his proudest achievements was the revitalization of Bienville Square in the mid-1980's
2 CONC , which he assisted by raising funds from the business community through Mobile's Streetscapes. N. Q. grew many beautiful camellias, enjoyed golfing at Mobile Country Club and loved hunting and fishing with his good friends at the
2 CONC  Bull Pen Hunting Club. He was predeceased by his parents Samuel Boyd Adams and Dora Willie Adams, brothers Samuel Boyd Adams Jr. and W. C. Adams, and his sister Ruth Adams, all of Mobile. A private internment was held Thursday at
2 CONC  Pinecrest Cemetery. A memorial service will be held noon Friday, October 12 at First Baptist Church of Mobile at 806 Government Street. In lieu of flowers, the family requests donations be made to the Foundation of First Baptist 
2 CONC Church.
2 CONT
