1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Dicy was the child of Thomas Goggans and Rebecca Teague.
2 CONT Dicy is the granddaughter of Thomas Bailey Johnston and Elizabeth Jane Dalrymple Johnston.
2 CONT is the great granddaughter of Thomas "Big" Johnston and Ann Lewis Johnston.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Thomas Goggans' death, Rebecca Teague, Dicy's mother, later married Jehu W. Pitts, the widower of Rebecca's deceased sister, Mary Teague Pitts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dicy was married to Nathaniel McCoy and had three children;
2 CONT William, Rebecca and John Teague McCoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of the 1850 census, Nathaniel was 60, a farmer, Dicy was 45, Rebecca, age 17, and John age 12.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dicy is my g'g'g'grandmother. According to the census her name is spelled with an "e", Dicey.
2 CONT In the book "Our Folks", by Sadie Goggans, the name Dicy is also spelled Dicey.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the 1850 census, Dicy had a brother, Elihu Goggans, a farmer, age 42, married to Elizabeth (?) and had two children Rebecca, age 18, and Gabriel, age 16. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the 1850 census, Elihu and Dicy's families lived next door on neighboring farms.
2 CONT
2 CONT The information in this entry was gained from the enumerated list dated 26 SEP 1850 Pontotoc, MS Free Inhabitants, Schedule I. The date of the census was 1 JUN 1850.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the book, "Our Folks", the dates for Dicy Goggans differ as follows:
2 CONT born 1799 died 19 OCT 1867.
2 CONT
