2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeremiah Brown is the third son of Rev. Chad Browne and Elizabeth Sharparowe.[1][2][3] The date of his birth is unknown. His elder brother, John, was born in 1630. Sources indicate John was the only child that accompanied his pare
2 CONC nts to America in 1638. Therefore as third child his birthday is estimated to be after 1639.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeremiah first married Mary who last name is unknown,[1][3] though the present Wikitree link to his first spouse presently identifies her maiden surname, without evidence, as Gardiner. His second wife was Mary (Sherman) Cook, the 
2 CONC widow of Thomas Cook.[1] They had one child James.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 6 September 1687 he is cited on Gov. Andros' tax roll at Rochester, (Kingstown) Rhode Island owing a “pole” (poll) tax of 1 s, and a property tax of 1s 2d. This tax record places him as an adult resident and as a property landh
2 CONC older in Kingstown, RI on this date. [4][3] This tax roll identifies a sum total of 136 heads of house living in the roughly 22.5 square mile area associated with Kingstown, RI on this date in 1687; one consequence of this sparse 
2 CONC population is that he would have been well acquainted with many, if not all, of the individuals identified on this list. The list is a defacto definition of the people who were available to be his friends, neighbors, and allies; i
2 CONC t would be essential to cultivate strong working relationships with these neighbors in order to survive on this frontier landscape. This cross reference tool provides hot links to peruse most of the 136 Wikitree families identifie
2 CONC d in this tax roll; families who were very frequently interconnected, or became interconnected, by marriage over the course of their lives and throughout the ensuing generations, further binding a network of neighbors into extende
2 CONC d families. [5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeremiah Brown died between 16 September and 30 October 1690,[1] and was buried in the Common Burying Ground, in Newport, Newport County, Rhode Island.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
