2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: The Chad Browne Memorial 
2 CONT Consisting of Genealogical Memoirs
2 CONT Of a Portion of the Descedants of Chad and Elizabeth Browne
2 CONT Compiled by a Descendant
2 CONT Printed for the Family
2 CONT Edition of Three Hundred Illustrated Copies, of which this Book is No. 209
2 CONT 1888 - Page 10
2 CONT John Brown (Chad 1), b. 1630, d. about 1706, m. Mary, daughter of Rev. Obadiah and Catherine Holmes of Newport, R.I. He resided in Providence at the North End, in the house afterwards occupied by his son, Elder James, near the jun
2 CONC ction of North Main and Randall Streets. We learn from the town records that he frequently served as Juryman, was commissioner of union of towns in 1654, and Freeman in 1665. Like his father he was a surveyor as well as Baptist El
2 CONC der, and in 1659 was appointed Surveyor of Highways. He served on various committees, was moderator, member of the Town Council, deputy in legislature, assistant, and took the oath of allegiance, May 31, 1666. In 1661 the town all
2 CONC owed a way that had been laid out across his land and other lots to be fenced under certain restrictions. It was afterwards laid out as Camp Street.
2 CONT In 1672, after the death of his mother, he sold the home lot of his father to his brother James of Newport, who re-sold it the same day to Daniel Abbot. The burial place of his parents, twenty feet square with free egress, was res
2 CONC erved. Nearly on hundred years later, his great grandsons, John and Moses Brown, repurchased a part of the this land, and presented it to the College of Rhode Island, at the time of its removal from Warren to Providence. On the 14
2 CONC th of May, 1770, the corner-stone of  University Hall, the first and for many years the only building, was laid by John Brown. In 1804, the name of the institution was changed to Brown University.
2 CONT In 1701 he and Pardon Tillinghast, elders of the church, ordained James Clarke as pastor of the Second Baptist Church in Newport.
