1 NAME Hans Jacob Jacob /Shuler/
2 GIVN Hans Jacob Jacob
2 SURN Shuler
2 _MARNM Schuler
2 NICK Johannes Schüller
1 NAME Jakob Schuler
1 SEX M
1 BAPM
2 DATE 13 JUL 1693
2 PLAC Ferenbalm Parish
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bibern
3 STAE Bern
3 CTRY Schweiz
1 BIRT
2 DATE 16 JUL 1693
2 PLAC Ferenbalm, Canton, Bern, Switzerland
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 21 FEB 1738
2 ADDR
3 STAE South Carolina
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Swiss records spell his name as "Jacob Shuler." (http://www.xs4all.nl/~sail/shuler/shuler4.html)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jakob moved from Switzerland to Germany sometime before 1718. The Swiss Canton of Bern adopted a policy that encouraged the emigration of landless people, Baptists and Anabaptists because these individuals were considered destabli
2 CONC zing to the Protestant and Catholic society. His brothers Hans Jeorg and Hans Heinrich also have moved to Germany and subsequently to the U.S. with him (http://www.rootcellar.us/shuler.htm).
2 CONT
2 CONT Jakob married Margaretha Lauer, and they had ten children born and baptized at the Lambsborn Reformed Church in Germany. Two children died in Germany, and the parents emigrated to South Carolina from Krahenberg with the remaining 
2 CONC eight in 1736.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was an aggressive program to attract Swiss and Germans to resettle in South Carolina in the 1730s (see http://www.ogsgs.org/recruit.htm). Each family was offered 50 acres for each man, woman and child in the family to immigr
2 CONC ate. The group that settled in South Carolina was led by a Rev. Giessendanner, who made several trips back and forth from Germany to South Carolina, helping more to come to this new land of opportunity. He paid for their boat pass
2 CONC age, with arrangements for the loan to be repaid later.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Swiss and German governments objected vehemently to the emigration of citizens and used harsh punitive means to try to block their departure (see http://tiny.cc/lpBww).
2 CONT
2 CONT Jakob was given 400 acres of land in Orangeburg Township in 1740, 50 acres for each of six daughters who made the journey, himself and his wife. Jakob died before the land was granted, so it was granted to his widow. (http://www.o
2 CONC gsgs.org/ffam/shuler.htm)
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Register Zweibrucken III No. 2025, for the year 1737, is the following remark: "Jakob Schuler of Krahenburg (Germany) moved to Carolina with wife and eight children." (Christening records from Germany indicate that Jacob ha
2 CONC d nine daughters and one son, but two of these died before the family left Germany, and three more died shortly after arriving in America." That would imply only five daughters reached adulthood, but six daughters are listed as ma
2 CONC rrying in Orangeburg, SC.
2 CONT
2 CONT (see (http://www.ogsgs.org/ffam/shuler.htm and http://www.xs4all.nl/~sail/shuler/shuler4.html)
2 CONT
2 CONT Other sources: http://www.xs4all.nl/~sail/orange/a-index.html
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT [It is] suggestive that Johan Jakob Schuler (Jacob's father-in-law) was baptized in Ferenbalm, Switzerland. Ferenbalm is listed as the only Wannemacher home village in Switzerland in the Eighteenth Century[7]. Unfortunately, I’ve 
2 CONC had no luck connecting Jacob Wannemacher to Ferenbalm. - jer
2 CONT
2 CONT Information provided by Jim Rickenbacker 7/14/08.
