1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/120605713/jacob-wannamaker Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Mar 4 2020, 0:46:16 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/120605713/jacob-wannamaker
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/K2YQ-Q7G
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob Wannamaker was born on 1777 Aug 25 in South Carolina's Orangeburg District to Jacob Wannamacker & Anna Rumph. His surname was originally anglicized from the German surname Wannenmacher, which means basketmaker. This family d
2 CONC escended from Wilhelm Wannenmacher, who received a coat-of-arms in 1555 from the emperor of Austria, and it included various German nobles and Protestant leaders. Jacob's immigrant grandfather Jacob Wannenmacher served in the Cher
2 CONC okee Indian War, and Jacob's father Jacob Wannamacker fought in the Revolutionary War.
2 CONT
2 CONT South Carolina in 1800 split Orangeburg District into both Orangeburg County and Barnwell County, and in 1804 split Orangeburg County to create Lexington County, but Jacob remained within Orangeburg County. Jacob was married about
2 CONC  1802 in South Carolina's Orangeburg County to Mary Magdalena Miller. Jacob & Mary resided in South Carolina's Orangeburg County. Jacob fathered 6 children with Mary, namely John, Mary, Ann, Selena, William, and Summerfield. John 
2 CONC would eventually become a reverend like his father, and would attend the Convention of the People of South Carolina in 1860 that signed South Carolina's Ordinance of Secession. Jacob became a widower about 1817.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob was remarried about 1817 in South Carolina's Orangeburg County to Rachel Whetstone. Jacob & Rachel resided in South Carolina's Orangeburg County. Jacob worked as a farmer and fathered at least 7 more children with Rachel, na
2 CONC mely Rachel, John, Jacob, Asbury, Marcus, Thomas, and Hayne. Jacob also served as a reverend. Jacob died on 1834 Sep 25 in South Carolina's Orangeburg County, and was buried afterward there in Wannamaker Family Cemetery. South Car
2 CONC olina in 1908 split Orangeburg County to create Calhoun County, which included Jacob's gravesite.
2 CONT
2 CONT
