1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Robert Livingston Massonneau, Sr.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/173858994/robert_livingston-massonneau
2 CONT * https://www.historicredhook.org/cemetery-crawl-robert-massonneau
2 CONT
2 CONT ROBERT LIVINGSTON MASSONNEAU, president of the First National Bank of Red Hook, Dutchess County, is a worthy representative of an ancestry which has long been prominent in the history of this region.
2 CONT
2 CONT His grandfather, Claudius Germain Massonneau, a native of Angouleme, France, left that country at the outbreak of the Revolution, with his twin brother Pierre, both being then in early manhood. They went to San Domingo, but upon t
2 CONC he insurrection of the negroes of that island, abandoned their plantation and fled to the U.S.
2 CONT
2 CONT After a short stay in New York City they settled, in 1790, in Red Hook, engaging in mercanitile enterprise. He married Catherine Gertrude Livingston de Rrissac, daughter of Robert Gilbert Livingston, and widow of M. de Brissac. Th
2 CONC ey had three children: Robert Claudius, Edward and Catherine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert C. Massonneau, the father of our subject, was born May 3, 1797, in Red Hook, and after enjoying such educational advantages as the schools of Red Hook then afforded, was sent to the Claverack Academy.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of 16 he began to assist his father in the village store, and soon after had control of the whole business, his father retiring to his farm, where he spent the remainder of his life, dying in 1846, in the 74th year of h
2 CONC is age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert C. added to his mercantile work the manufacture of cigars and tobacco, which venture proved highly successful from its inception.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Elizabeth Waldorf, daughter of John Waldorf, a leading citizen of Red Hook in 1824, and reared a family of children whose names are as follows: Robert Livingston (our subject), born October 3, 1825; Catherine Elizabeth 
2 CONC (now widow of David Sparks); Peter Neilson; Charles Waldorf; Edward Francis; and Mary Alice (now Mrs. LeGrand B. Curtis).
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert L. Massonneau attended the schools of his native place, entering while young his father's store as clerk, serving in that capacity a number of years.
2 CONT
2 CONT On October 27, 1859, he married Emma Clark Strobel, who was born December 19, 1859, in New York City. She was a daughter of Rev. William D. Strobel, D.D., at that time pastor of St. Paul's Lutheran Church of Red Hook.
2 CONT
2 CONT Five children were born of this union: Robert Livingston, Jr.; William Strobel; Edward Philip; Eliza Franklin and Emma Engs.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1852 our subject engaged in the tobacco business, established by his father, continuing therein until 1S67, when he sold out his interest. His trained business talent and sound judgment have made him a recognized force in busin
2 CONC ess circles.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the organization of the First National Bank of Red Hook, in 1865, he was chosen director and afterward vice-president.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the death of the president, William Chamberlain, he was elected to that position, which he has since tilled with distinguished ability. In his political preferences he was originally a Democrat, but since the formation of the p
2 CONC arty has been a Republican.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Massonneau, wife of our subject, was born in New York City, where her father at the time was pastor of St. James Lutheran Church. He was born May 17, 1808, at Charleston, South Carolina, and married Abby Anna Engs, who was bo
2 CONC rn in New York City September 17, 1815.
2 CONT
2 CONT They had seven children, four of whom are yet living, Mrs. Massoneau being the third in order of birth. Her father died December 6, 1884, in Rhinebeck, New York, and, with his wife and three children, is buried in the cemetery at 
2 CONC Red Hook.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Strobel, great-grandfather of Mrs. Massonneau, owned a large tannery in Charleston, South Carolina, about the middle of the 18th century, serving in the war of the Revolution as a captain in the "German Fusileers," the famed 
2 CONC regiment of South Carolina. Her great grandfather, Colonel Jonas Beard, also served in the Revolution.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Commemorative biographical record of Dutchess County, New York by J.H. Beers & Co Publication date 1897 Topics Dutchess County (N.Y.) -- Biography, Genealogy Publisher Chicago, J.H. Beers & Co.
