1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/132084307/john-edward-fitts
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN EDWARD FITTS, SR. JULY 4, 1914 – SEPTEMBER 4 2007 GASTON, SC
2 CONT
2 CONT John Edward Fitts, Sr., of Gaston, SC, died on September 4, 2007. Funeral services were held at 11 AM Thursday, September 6, 2007 in First Baptist Church of Columbia, SC, Boyce Chapel. Interment will follow at Crescent Hill Memori
2 CONC al Gardens directed by Thompson Funeral Home of Lexington. Visitation will be held one hour prior to service at the church. Pallbearers will be grandsons, John Fitts, III, Ted Fitts, Lee Dutton, Joel Dutton, Thomas Fitts, Kell Fit
2 CONC ts, Daniel Fitts, and Steven Fitts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Fitts was born on July 4, 1914, in Beaufort, and grew up in Brunson. He was the son of the late Robert Raymond and Beulah (Harley) Fitts and the husband of the late Delia Elizabeth (Kell) Fitts (1919-2004). Mr. & Mrs. Fitts ha
2 CONC d been married for sixty-six years. Mr. Fitts was a member of the First Baptist Church of Columbia. He served as a deacon and was a member of the Baraca Class.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Fitts served in the United States Army Air Corps, attended the University of South Carolina, was a Base Engineer at Columbia Army Air Base during WW II, presently the Columbia Metropolitan Airport, and was a pioneer in the dev
2 CONC elopment of the sign industry. He was co-founder of Colonial-Hites Company in 1950 when his own company merged with Hites Manufacturing Company. He was co-founder of Colite Industries and retired in 1973 as CEO and Chairman of the
2 CONC  Board. He was co-founder of Concept Unlimited in which he retired in 2003 as President.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his sign career many of today's standard techniques in the sign industry were developed by Mr. Fitts. He was the first to successfully produce the welded aluminum letter with a plastic face. Utilizing knowledge gained from 
2 CONC his experience with aircraft aluminum at Columbia Army Air Base during World War II, he conceived the idea for using aluminum to replace steel letters, and aluminum became the standard of the industry because of its longevity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Surviving are sons, John E. Fitts, Jr. and wife, Mary Claire of Gaston; Thomas M. Fitts and wife, Peggy of Columbia; Steven R. Fitts and wife, Rosemary of Lexington; a daughter, Mary F. Dutton and husband, Roy of Gaston; eleven gr
2 CONC andchildren; seventeen great-grandchildren; brothers, George Harley Fitts (1919-2010) of West Columbia, Ralph R. Fitts and wife, Marie of Charleston; a sister, Lydia Lorene (Fitts) McManus (1909-2010) of Gafney; and special friend
2 CONC s, Jack and Carol Coker and Bob and Emily Newell
2 CONT
2 CONT Father, Robert Raymond Fitts (1883-1952), Mother, Beulah Ernest (Harley) Fitts (1884-1972), Brothers, Glenn Louis Fitts (1912-1997) and Walton Reid Fitts (1928-1991), sisters, Raye (Fitts) Kull (d. May 31, 1998), Beulah Rosemond (
2 CONC Fitts) Morrison McIntosh (1916-1984), and Dora Jeanette (Fitts) Young (1912-1997), brothers-in-law, Harry Ketley Morrison (1893-1972) and Dr. Clifton Brooke McIntosh, Sr. (1905-1979), and nephew, Robert H. Morrison (1952-1992), pr
2 CONC edeceased him.
